---
title: Basics
order: 1
description: Repository setup, staging, commits, and status
tags: [git, init, clone, add, commit, status, gitignore]
---

Core workflow: init or clone a repo, stage changes, commit them.

@code bash
# Initialize a new repository
git init
git init my-project              # create repo in new directory

# Clone an existing repository
git clone https://github.com/user/repo.git
git clone git@github.com:user/repo.git      # SSH
git clone --depth 1 <url>                   # shallow clone (latest commit only)
git clone --branch v2.0 <url>               # clone specific branch/tag
git clone --recurse-submodules <url>         # include submodules

@text
Staging controls what goes into the next commit. The index (staging area)
sits between the working tree and the repository.

@code bash
# Check working tree status
git status
git status -s                    # short format (XY path)
git status -sb                   # short with branch info

# Stage files
git add file.go                  # stage single file
git add src/ lib/                # stage directories
git add -A                       # stage all changes (new, modified, deleted)
git add -p                       # interactive hunk staging
git add -u                       # stage modified/deleted only (no new files)

# Unstage files
git restore --staged file.go     # remove from staging (keep changes)
git reset HEAD file.go           # same thing (older syntax)

@text
Commits record staged changes. Write clear, imperative-mood messages.

@code bash
# Commit staged changes
git commit -m "add user auth module"
git commit                       # open $EDITOR for message
git commit -am "fix typo"        # stage tracked files + commit (skips untracked)
git commit --amend               # modify last commit (message and/or content)
git commit --amend --no-edit     # amend content, keep message
git commit --allow-empty -m "trigger CI"

# Show last commit
git log -1
git show HEAD

@text
The .gitignore file tells git which files to ignore. Patterns are matched
against paths relative to the .gitignore location.

@code bash
# Common .gitignore patterns
*.o                              # compiled objects
*.log                            # log files
build/                           # build output directory
.env                             # environment secrets
node_modules/                    # dependency directories
__pycache__/                     # Python bytecode cache

# Negate a pattern (include something otherwise ignored)
!important.log

# Ignore only in root directory
/TODO

# Check why a file is ignored
git check-ignore -v path/to/file

# Global gitignore (user-wide)
git config --global core.excludesFile ~/.gitignore_global

# Stop tracking a file without deleting it
git rm --cached file.txt
git rm -r --cached dir/          # recursive
